<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matilda of Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1031–1083</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matilda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> was the daughter of Baldwin V, count of Flanders, and the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, whom she married around 1051. She governed Normandy during his absences in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and was crowned queen of England in 1068. With William she founded the two great abbeys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a>, and she was buried in her own foundation, the Abbaye aux Dames.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matilda-of-flanders.jpg" alt="Matilda of Flanders" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tosca · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luxembourg_-_La_Reine_Mathilde.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
